  "slug": "quantum-algorithm-f265",
  "summary": "Seven preprints report lower gate and qubit costs for chemistry, PDEs and combinatorial optimization on fault-tolerant and near-term hardware.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is resource reduction through problem-specific structure. For fault-tolerant chemistry, a sum-of-squares Hamiltonian in first quantisation yields up to 16x fewer Toffoli gates, while canonical polyadic decomposition of Coulomb-like potentials on real-space grids lowers T-gate scaling and qubit counts. An end-to-end algorithm treats the variable-coefficient linearized Poisson-Boltzmann equation for a biomolecular system. A quantum-classical framework uses simulated annealing and quantum-assisted ground-state estimation to find compact many-fermion bases. On near-term hardware, FLICD outperforms COBYLA in simulation for variational circuit optimization, QUBO and HOBO encodings of the traveling salesman problem are compared, and QSSD uses fixed-angle QAOA sampling on IBM devices up to 152 qubits, reaching 0.95 approximation on benchmarks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
